## Enums: One Type, Multiple Choices

An **enum** (enumeration) defines a type that can be exactly one of several named **variants**. Think of it as a closed set of possible values.

### Defining an Enum

```xiom
pub type Suit = enum {
  Clubs,
  Diamonds,
  Hearts,
  Spades,
}
```

Each variant represents a distinct possibility — a `Suit` value must be one of these four.

### Creating Enum Values

```xiom
let s = Suit.Hearts;
let trump = Suit.Spades;
```

Use `TypeName.Variant` to construct an enum value.

### Enums in Functions

```xiom
fn suit_symbol(suit: &Suit) -> Str {
  match suit {
    Clubs => { return "♣"; }
    Diamonds => { return "♢"; }
    Hearts => { return "♡"; }
    Spades => { return "♠"; }
  }
}
```

### Derive with Enums

Enums can derive traits just like structs:

```xiom
pub type Day = enum {
  Monday,
  Tuesday,
  Wednesday,
  Thursday,
  Friday,
  Saturday,
  Sunday,
} derive[Eq, Clone, Debug]
```

### Why Enums?

Enums prevent invalid states at compile time. Instead of using a string (`if day == "Munday"`) or number (`if day == 8`), the compiler guarantees the value is always a valid variant.

### Comparing Enum Variants

```xiom
pub type Status = enum { Active, Inactive, } derive[Eq]

let a = Status.Active;
let b = Status.Inactive;
a == b;  // false
a == Status.Active;  // true
```

